  data:  # 456f
    kind: invariant  # 456f
    title: "Swift emits the receiver VARIABLE NAME in the module slot, so the hint is a local identifier rather than a type"  # 456f
    status: violated  # 456f
    priority: 3  # 456f
    parent:  # 456f
    tags:  # 456f
    - analysis_quality  # 456f
    - io_boundary  # 456f
    - cross_language_parity  # 456f
    isbefore: []  # 456f
    duplicate_of: []  # 456f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 456f
    pr_ref:  # 456f
    description: "Swift puts the RECEIVER VARIABLE NAME where every other analyzer puts a module\nor type, so the module slot carries a local identifier rather than evidence.\n\nOBSERVED (probe run during the WI-zilag / PR #247 measurement sweep): a Swift\ncall on a local named `fm` emitted\n\n    swift:fm:0-0:fileExists\n\n`fm` is a variable name, not a module and not a type. The io-boundary and taint\nmatchers both read that slot as a MODULE HINT and filter catalogue entries with\n`_module_matches` against it. A variable name can only ever produce a spurious\nmatch or, far more often, silence -- and because a present-but-mismatched hint\nis REFUSED outright (it never reaches gate_named_entry's no-hint path), this\nalso suppresses matches that the untyped path would otherwise have allowed.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND SWIFT: the swift catalogue holds 104 primitives, 97 of\nthem method-kind. If the slot is a variable name for method calls generally,\nthat catalogue is largely unreachable, the same shape kotlin is in (WI-nasuf).\n\nWHY IT IS FILED RATHER THAN FIXED HERE: PR #247 was python-only and deliberately\nso. Swift was surveyed as a candidate second language and DISQUALIFIED -- not\nbecause it lacks the shape (URL.appendingPathComponent IS type-preserving in the\nlanguage) but because every catalogued swift primitive takes the URL/path as an\nARGUMENT, so there is no receiver to type. That disqualification is independent\nof this defect.\n\nSTATUS OF THE EVIDENCE, stated honestly: this is ONE probe observation from a\nsweep whose verifiers did not run (the workflow hit a usage limit). It has NOT\nbeen reproduced independently, the population has NOT been sized, and no repo\nsurvey was done. The FIRST action on this item is to reproduce the emission on a\nreal Swift repo and size how many method-kind call sites carry a variable name\nin the module slot. Do not act on the 104/97 catalogue figures as if they were\na measured blast radius -- they are catalogue counts, not site counts.\n\nRELATED: kotlin emits no unresolved method-call edge for a receiver method call\nat all (WI-nasuf). Both were found the same way -- a positive control that\nFAILED, which is what distinguishes \"measured zero\" from \"instrument null\"."  # 456f
    fields:  # 456f
      statement: "A call edge's module slot MUST carry a module or type identity, never a local variable name. A consumer reading it as a module hint cannot distinguish the two, and a present-but-wrong hint is refused outright rather than degrading to the no-hint path."  # 456f
      root_cause: "UNCONFIRMED. Observed once: a swift call on a local named 'fm' emitted swift:fm:0-0:fileExists. The emission site in the swift analyzer has not been located and the population has not been sized."  # 456f
- op: discuss  # cf31
  at: '2026-09-01T05:46:46Z'  # cf31
  by: agent  # cf31
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cf31
  clock: 2  # cf31
  nonce: cf31  # cf31
  message: "ENRICHMENT from the module-key concept audit, 2026-09-01 (cadence). Full write-up:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-module-key_09012026.md\n\nTHIS ITEM'S root_cause SAYS \"UNCONFIRMED. Observed once... The emission site in the\nswift analyzer has not been located and the population has not been sized.\"\nCONFIRMED, SIZED, AND ONE EMISSION SITE LOCATED.\n\nPOPULATION, measured on the objc+swift repos of a 21-repo cold cohort\n(AFNetworking, fmdb, CocoaLumberjack, Alamofire, swift-argument-parser):\n\n    module slots starting LOWERCASE (receiver-variable shaped): 1,102 refs / 266 distinct\n    module slots starting UPPERCASE (type shaped)             : 3,105 refs / 413 distinct\n\nSo 26% of objc+swift external refs carry a local identifier where every other\nanalyzer puts a module or type. Most frequent: expectation x164, request x76,\ncompletionReceived x48, expect x40, eventMonitor x32, session x32, expectation1 x27,\ncontinuation x22, send x20, resp x20, mutableState x16, responses x16, download x16,\ncomponents x14.\n\nEMISSION SITE, objc: objc.py passes `module_path=receiver_name` directly into the\nExternalRef. That is the objc half stated literally in code; the swift half still\nneeds its own site located (swift.py has gate_path_hint / path_hint forms), so this\nenrichment does NOT close that part of the item.\n\nTHE MATCHER ALREADY ENCODES THE DEFECT AS A SUPPORTED CASE, which is the part worth\nkeeping. _module_matches carries an explicit Swift carve-out whose docstring says:\n\"Swift hints are receiver *variable* names (camelCase) against PascalCase catalog\ntypes, and the variable is often the type's trailing word -- fileIO for\nNonBlockingFileIO.\" So the predicate was extended to match variable names against\ntypes rather than the emission being fixed. The carve-out is direction-restricted\n(catalog must END WITH hint, never the reverse) precisely because the reverse\ndirection is the `chaos` ends with `os` bug.\n\nRELATED, NOT DUPLICATE: INV-dijor (filed today) is the same family from the other\nside -- there a catalogue module ENDING in a TYPE matches a bare lowercase hint via\n_module_matches arm 3, verified live on go/sops. This item is the producer side\n(the slot holds a variable name); INV-dijor is the matcher side (the predicate\naccepts one). Fixing either alone leaves the other live."  # cf31
