  message: "ENRICHMENT from the module-key concept audit, 2026-09-01 (cadence). Full write-up:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-module-key_09012026.md\n\nTHIS ITEM'S root_cause SAYS \"UNCONFIRMED. Observed once... The emission site in the\nswift analyzer has not been located and the population has not been sized.\"\nCONFIRMED, SIZED, AND ONE EMISSION SITE LOCATED.\n\nPOPULATION, measured on the objc+swift repos of a 21-repo cold cohort\n(AFNetworking, fmdb, CocoaLumberjack, Alamofire, swift-argument-parser):\n\n    module slots starting LOWERCASE (receiver-variable shaped): 1,102 refs / 266 distinct\n    module slots starting UPPERCASE (type shaped)             : 3,105 refs / 413 distinct\n\nSo 26% of objc+swift external refs carry a local identifier where every other\nanalyzer puts a module or type. Most frequent: expectation x164, request x76,\ncompletionReceived x48, expect x40, eventMonitor x32, session x32, expectation1 x27,\ncontinuation x22, send x20, resp x20, mutableState x16, responses x16, download x16,\ncomponents x14.\n\nEMISSION SITE, objc: objc.py passes `module_path=receiver_name` directly into the\nExternalRef. That is the objc half stated literally in code; the swift half still\nneeds its own site located (swift.py has gate_path_hint / path_hint forms), so this\nenrichment does NOT close that part of the item.\n\nTHE MATCHER ALREADY ENCODES THE DEFECT AS A SUPPORTED CASE, which is the part worth\nkeeping. _module_matches carries an explicit Swift carve-out whose docstring says:\n\"Swift hints are receiver *variable* names (camelCase) against PascalCase catalog\ntypes, and the variable is often the type's trailing word -- fileIO for\nNonBlockingFileIO.\" So the predicate was extended to match variable names against\ntypes rather than the emission being fixed. The carve-out is direction-restricted\n(catalog must END WITH hint, never the reverse) precisely because the reverse\ndirection is the `chaos` ends with `os` bug.\n\nRELATED, NOT DUPLICATE: INV-dijor (filed today) is the same family from the other\nside -- there a catalogue module ENDING in a TYPE matches a bare lowercase hint via\n_module_matches arm 3, verified live on go/sops. This item is the producer side\n(the slot holds a variable name); INV-dijor is the matcher side (the predicate\naccepts one). Fixing either alone leaves the other live."  # 499b
- op: discuss  # 723c
  at: '2026-09-01T20:13:50Z'  # 723c
  by: agent  # 723c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 723c
  clock: 4  # 723c
  nonce: 723c  # 723c
  message: "SWIFT'S EMISSION SITE IS LOCATED. This item was carried as \"objc site located,\nswift's not\"; it is swift.py:1223, inside the INV-pirot method-call gate:\n\n    gate_path_hint = (\n        receiver_type                       # a TYPE      (var_types lookup)\n        or import_aliases.get(callee_name)  # a NAMESPACE (import alias)\n        or receiver_hint                    # a receiver VARIABLE\n        or \"external\"                       # a SENTINEL\n    )\n    ... ExternalRef(lang=\"swift\", module_path=gate_path_hint, name=callee_name)\n\nONE EXPRESSION, FOUR DIFFERENT NOTIONS, IN PRIORITY ORDER, ALL WRITTEN INTO ONE\nSLOT WITH NO RECORD OF WHICH ONE PRODUCED IT. This is the module-key conflation\nin its most literal form and is now the best single piece of evidence for\nWI-kijup's axiom -- better than the corpus percentages, because it is four lines\nof source rather than a measurement. Supporting reads: receiver_hint is\nreceiver_parts[0] (swift.py:1037), the first component of the receiver\nexpression, so a variable spelling; var_types is populated from variable\ndeclarations (:1108) and parameters (:1117), so receiver_type is a resolved type.\n\nThe second swift site (:1299) is NOT this shape: path_hint there is\nimport_aliases.get(callee_name) alone, a namespace, and the ExternalRef is\nwithheld entirely when it is absent. Only the gate site conflates.\n\nA CORRECTION TO WHAT THE AUDIT RECORDED ABOUT OBJC, since it changes the\nmigration target. The audit said objc \"writes module_path=receiver_name\nliterally\", and it does (objc.py:644) -- but the site is GATED on\nreceiver_name[0].isupper() (:637), and a capitalised Objective-C message\nreceiver is normally a CLASS name, not a variable. So objc's slot is\npredominantly a TYPE, not a receiver variable, and the lowercase identifiers the\naudit counted (expectation, request, session, resp, download, components) cannot\nhave come from that site. The receiver-VARIABLE notion is SWIFT's, via the third\nclause above. The \"26% of objc+swift refs\" figure was over the combined cohort\nand should not be attributed to objc.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR WI-marok (the gated Phase 2 migration): its objc bullet is\nmis-aimed as filed and should be re-derived per-language before any row moves.\nRecorded there too."  # 723c
